{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>The split-hoofed.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Ḥullin: it is a creature that has two backs and two spines, and it brings up the cud. And why were the animals repeated here, seeing they were already stated in Torat Kohanim in the section of ויהי ביום השמיני? They were repeated only on account of the שסועה; and likewise the birds were repeated only on account of the ראה, for in Torat Kohanim he did not mention the שסועה nor the ראה; and there he mentioned (Leviticus 11) ״דאה,״ and ראה and דאה are all one. And why is its name called ראה? Because it sees exceedingly — it stands here and sees carrion in the land of Israel. And likewise איה and דיה are one; and because it has two names, Scripture wrote them all, lest some people eat this one whose name is איה and some eat this one whose name is דיה. And as for why he specified the unclean birds — because it is revealed and known before Him that the clean birds are more numerous than the unclean ones, therefore he wrote the unclean ones and specified the few. From here they said: a person should always teach his student the shorter way. And it is called שלך because it fishes (שולה) fish from the sea; and דוכיפת — its meaning is that its crest is doubled (כפולה), and it is the wild rooster."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:16.511797592Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}